  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nWraparound rain from the Nor'easter spinning off the New Jersey\ncoastline is sliding down the west side of the ridges at this\ntime. POPs have been raised slightly a little further west to\ncatch this. Early looks at the 00z Pittsburgh RAOB show less \ndry air at the surface than previously expected, this is showing\nup in DUJ obs, where reports are already up to 0.12 inches of\nrain. Light rain trails off after this band passes and POPs drop\nappreciably overnight. Widespread boundary layer gusts between\n20-30 mph are beginning to wind down as the boundary layer\ndecouples.\n\nRain chances decrease later tonight as the nor'easter starts to\nwaddle northeastward towards Cape Cod, but a few upslope showers\nmay linger along the ridges southeast of Pittsburgh as surface\nwind backs slightly towards northwest. Clouds should linger \ninto Monday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAs the coastal trough associated with the nor'easter pulls \noffshore, 500mb ridging will be able to build across the Upper \nOhio Valley by 12Z Wednesday. Dry weather will result into \nThursday, with that latter day representing the peak of a \nwarming trend as southwest flow amps up behind a Carolina \nsurface high. NBM probabilities for 80F or greater high \ntemperatures that day have risen to between 50 and 80 percent \nfor most non-ridge locations south of I-80. That said, dewpoints\ntopping out in the low 60s will keep heat risk concerns \nrelatively muted. Currently, probabilities for moderate or \nhigher heat risk levels are 10 percent or less.\n\nThere are differences in opinion in the longer-range models\nregarding the strength and timing of the next shortwave trough\ncrossing the Canadian prairies and potentially the Great Lakes\nlater in the week. Still, at least scattered shower activity \nbecomes more likely Thursday night/Friday in the moistening warm\nsector, with rain chances lingering into the weekend. Severe \nweather potential looks muted by meager instability, and \nprobabilities for significant rain are low (40 percent less or \nchance for >0.50 inch in any 24-hour period). Given the\nincreased clouds and rain chances, temperatures should tumble \nback to, or perhaps a bit below, climatology by Saturday.",
